[38;2;124;124;124m_Y}$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, what is $\epsilon_{\mathcal{O}_X} \circ f^{-1}(f^\sharp)$?[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a morphism $f^{-1}\mathcal{O}_Y \to \mathcal{O}_X$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy the adjunction, the adjoint of $f^\sharp: \mathcal{O}_Y \to f_*\mathcal{O}_X$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mis exactly $\epsilon_{\mathcal{O}_X} \circ f^{-1}(f^\sharp): f^{-1}\mathcal{O}_Y[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\to \mathcal{O}_X$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a standard fact about adjunctions: the adjoint (transpose) of a morphism[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$g: A \to f_*B$ (where $A = \mathcal{O}_Y, B = \mathcal{O}_X$) is $\epsilon_B [0m
